213 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 505 0 R
/ActualText (Procedural programming is a programming paradigm built around the idea that programs are sequences of instructions to be executed. )
/Alt ()
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
214 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 506 0 R
/ActualText (They focus heavily on splitting up programs into named sets of instructions called procedures, analogous to functions.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
215 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 507 0 R
/ActualText (The programming language C is an example of a language that is purely procedural.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 508 0 R
/ActualText ( However, many programming languages support multiple paradigms. For example Python, which is a popular general-purpose programming language, allows you to code using procedural, object-oriented, or functional techniques.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
217 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Main Characteristics of Procedural Programming)
/E (Main Characteristics of Procedural Programming)
/Alt ()
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
218 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 509 0 R
/ActualText (Procedural programming is distinct from other programming paradigms in three ways: it uses loops instead of recursion, and its code allows for data mutation.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 510 0 R
/ActualText (Use of loops: Loops, such as for loops, while loops, or do-while loops, are commonly used to repeat a set of instructions until a specific condition is met. Loops are essential in procedural programming.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
220 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 511 0 R
/ActualText (Absence \(or reduced presence\) of recursion: While recursion is entirely possible in procedural programming, it is used less frequently than in other types of programming, such as functional programming. Loops are typically preferred over recursion in procedural programming.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
221 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 512 0 R
/ActualText (Emphasis on data mutation: In procedural programming, data is often mutable, meaning it can be modified, allowing the programmer to process the data and update variables as needed by the program.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
222 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Benefits)
/E (Benefits)
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
223 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 513 0 R
/ActualText (Splitting code into smaller chunks has many benefits:)
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
224 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 514 0 R
/ActualText (It is much easier to test and debug, e.g. tracing 20 lines of code instead of 200 lines of code.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 515 0 R
/ActualText (Subroutines can be called many times, reducing the amount of repeated code.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 516 0 R
/ActualText (Subroutines can manipulate shared data.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 517 0 R
/ActualText (Subroutines can be saved in libraries and imported into programs when they are needed. This means that code is reusable; it's good if you can write code once and make use of it many times.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 518 0 R
/ActualText (Very large programs can be worked on by a whole team of programmers who can develop subroutines concurrently \(at the same time\) so that a project can be completed faster.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Benefits)
/E (Benefits)
/Alt ()
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
230 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 519 0 R
/ActualText (The code is reusable: One of the primary benefits of procedural programming is the ability to reuse code in different parts of the program without needing to rewrite it. Once a procedure is written, it can be called as many times as needed, resulting in simpler and more readable code.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 520 0 R
/ActualText (The code is clearer: Breaking down a program into procedures makes the code clearer and easier to understand. Each procedure has a specific role, assisting developers in following their program more easily and effectively.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 521 0 R
/ActualText (Code errors are more easily corrected: By isolating specific tasks in procedures, developers can test and debug each part of the program independently. In case of an error in a program organized with procedures, a developer can locate and correct the error more quickly than in poorly organized code, leading to significant time and efficiency savings.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
233 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Drawbacks )
/E (Drawbacks )
/Alt ()
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
234 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 522 0 R
/ActualText (One criticism of the procedural paradigm is that it focuses on what needs to be done, rather than on the integrity of the data that it manipulates.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
235 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 523 0 R
/ActualText (Another factor that can cause issues is that in the procedural paradigm it is possible to use global variables. The problem with global variables is that, since they can be accessed and modified by every subroutine in a program, it is really hard to work out where a value is changed. If you are trying to debug a program, you may have to consider every subroutine that modifies the global state. That is possible, but with a really large application, it is very difficult to do.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
236 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 524 0 R
/ActualText (Of course, the use of global variables is not a necessary component of the procedural paradigm, but it is allowed. Global variables should always be used very carefully. Indeed, there is nothing in the procedural paradigm that truly protects data. Data is passed into subroutines, used, and sometimes modified, and the programmer must be fully aware of what they should and shouldn't do, and abide by the rules of good practice to ensure data integrity.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
237 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Functional Programming Paradigm)
/E (Functional Programming Paradigm)
/Alt ()
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
